    that lives as long as the page. You lose persistence, not function.
  - [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m needs a secure context and isn't guaranteed on [38;5;153mfile://[39m. Fallback chain: [38;5;153mrandomUUID()[39m →
    [38;5;153mcrypto.getRandomValues()[39m formatted as hex → a [38;5;153mMath.random()[39m-based ID. The last one is weak, and that's acceptable
    here [3monly[0m because this handle is a correlation label and never a secret — I'll say so in the comment so nobody
    reuses the helper for a token.
  - Worst case, everything fails: [38;5;153mgetHandle()[39m returns a per-page ID, [38;5;153mtrack[39m still logs, login behaves exactly as it does
    today.

  [1mTesting.[0m There's no test runner in this repo, which brings up tooling — the cheapest moment to decide is now, before
  the code exists. My recommendation is unit tests via Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode --test[39m: it needs zero dependencies, which
  keeps [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m as dependency-free as it is today. The test file stubs [38;5;153mwindow[39m, the two storages, and [38;5;153mcrypto[39m, and
  covers: the handle mints once and is stable across a simulated reload; [38;5;153msetUserId[39m/[38;5;153mgetUserId[39m/clear; a throwing storage
  falls back to memory instead of propagating; and [38;5;153mtrack[39m output always carries the handle and never carries a password.

  Linting would mean adding the repo's first dependencies, and end-to-end or fuzz testing is out of proportion to four
  functions — but they're your call, so pick what you want set up.

  Either way I'll also do one manual browser pass: submit the form, confirm the log line carries a handle, reload and
  confirm the handle is unchanged, then reopen the tab and confirm [38;5;153muserId[39m is gone but the handle survived.
